From e64c6b07755e5f8fb74e7a3a74bc5d835ae05517 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Allen <cma@bitemyapp.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 19:55:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Rasterize scroll tiles between frames

The render thread rasterized each tile paint as its message arrived. A
display pass sends the tile it draws ahead of a scroll right after the
frame's present, so a 5 to 15 ms rasterization sat in front of the
compositor's next frame callback and the next present: a dropped frame
for each new tile while scrolling on a 120 Hz display.

A tile now keeps the paints it gets, pending, and the render thread
rasterizes pending paints oldest first while nothing else waits. While
any are left it looks for events without sleeping and rasterizes a 2 ms
slice after handling them, so frame callbacks and presents come first. A
present finishes the pending paints of the tiles it shows before
uploading them, so no frame shows a tile half drawn. A slice ends between
operations and never inside a group, whose layer lives only while one run
of the rasterizer draws it, so a tile holds exactly what rasterizing it
at once would have left; a unit test paints tiles with fills, paths,
nested groups and a shadow, rasterizes them an operation at a time, and
compares every pixel with painting them at once.

SIDESTEP_TRACE_FRAMES now reports what each present spent on its layers
and on finishing tiles, the time spent rasterizing between frames, and
which message was the slowest; SIDESTEP_TRACE_FRAMES=tiles also lists
each tile a present had to finish and each text view damaging itself
after layout.

